# ${vault_id} — an LLM-Wiki vault

This repository is **one Obsidian vault** that follows the [Karpathy LLM-Wiki
pattern](https://gist.github.com/karpathy/442a6bf555914893e9891c11519de94f).
It is not a RAG dump — it is a **persistent, compounding knowledge base**:
every source we ingest is read once, distilled, cross-referenced into existing
pages, and then *kept current* — so future questions answer from a synthesis
that already exists, not from raw transcripts.

Claude is launched at the vault root. All paths below are relative to it.

> ## ⚠️ SEARCH-FIRST — the one rule that makes this a wiki
> Before you answer **ANY** question that touches this vault's subject matter — a
> how-to ("how do I X?"), a definition ("what is X?"), or any topic the user may have
> notes on — you **MUST** run `/wiki-search` **first**. Do **NOT** answer from your own
> training knowledge until you have searched and confirmed the vault has nothing
> relevant (broaden once on 0 hits — content words / prefix `*` / spelling variants —
> before concluding it's empty).
> - **Vault has relevant pages** → ground the answer in them and **cite** the `[^src-<slug>]`.
> - **Vault has nothing** → say so explicitly ("the wiki has no page on X") *before* any
>   general-knowledge answer, label it **NOT from the vault**, and offer `/wiki-import`
>   to capture a real external source (a URL, PDF, transcript, or a local raw file).
>
> Answering a domain question from training **without searching first** — or presenting
> training knowledge as if it came from the vault — defeats the entire point of a
> compounding knowledge base. A smaller/faster model (e.g. Haiku) is **less reliable**
> at honouring this; prefer a stronger model when grounded answers matter.

- **vault_id**: `${vault_id}` (REQUIRED, ADR-002 §D1.1; defined in `WIKI_SCHEMA.md`)
- **language**: `${language}`
- **layout**: `${layout}` — one of `karpathy`/`flat`/`per-project` (the
  `_sources/_concepts/_entities` tiers below), `dev-project` (a code-repo `docs/`
  tree), or `obsidian-personal` (a real PARA vault: numbered folders, MOCs,
  multilingual names). Tune per-vault via `<vault>/.wiki/layout.yaml` (extend
  `ignore`) + `<vault>/.wiki/sync.yaml` (sync zones) — see `WIKI_SCHEMA.md`.

---

## The three layers (Karpathy architecture)

Keep them strictly separated:

1. **Raw sources** — *immutable*. Original transcripts (`lesson.txt`), articles,
   notes. Never edited after capture; the LLM may re-read them anytime.
2. **The wiki** — *LLM-owned*. `_sources/`, `_concepts/`, `_entities/`,
   `index.md`, `log.md`. Claude creates, updates, cross-links. Humans read and
   browse via `[[wiki-links]]` in Obsidian.
3. **The schema** — *co-evolved*. `WIKI_SCHEMA.md` governs conventions and
   layout. This `CLAUDE.md` ties operations to schema. Both adjust as the
   workflow matures.

> Obsidian is the IDE. Claude is the programmer. The wiki is the codebase.

---

## Layout

```
${vault_id}/                                    # ← Obsidian vault root
├── CLAUDE.md                                   # ← you are here
├── WIKI_SCHEMA.md                              # vault_id + schema conventions
├── _sources/                                   # one page per ingested source (NEVER promoted)
├── _concepts/                                  # canonical concept pages
├── _entities/                                  # entities (people / orgs / products)
├── _raw/                                       # raw imports + .locks/ + failed/
├── 00-Vault-Index/
│   ├── index.md                                # auto-generated catalog (read-only projection)
│   └── log/YYYY-MM.md                          # append-only monthly journal
└── Lessons/                                    # per-project ONLY: two-tier vaults
    └── <Course-Name>/                          # each course has its own wiki
        ├── WIKI_SCHEMA.md                      # course-local schema
        ├── _sources/                           # one page per source for this course
        ├── _concepts/                          # course-local concepts
        └── _entities/                          # course-local entities
```

For `per-project` (two-tier) layout: each `Lessons/<Course>/` carries its own
`_sources/`, `_concepts/`, `_entities/`. The **root** `_concepts/` /
`_entities/` is a *shared* layer populated lazily when a concept appears in
≥ 2 courses and an operator confirms they describe the same thing
(promotion — see `WIKI_SCHEMA.md`).

For `flat` (single-tier): only the root tier exists; no `Lessons/` directory.

---

## Operations

Two layers of tooling, complementary:

- **Construct layer (`/wiki-import` + `/wiki-sync`)** — turn a source into a
  compounding node. **`/wiki-import`** is the *unified* path and per-source engine:
  hand it ONE source — a URL, PDF, office doc, meeting transcript, X-thread, finished
  summary, **or a local raw file** — and it does deterministic fetch+convert → REASON
  (the `summarizing-meetings` harness, fed the vault's `known_concepts`) → a summary
  note **plus** its `_concepts/` pages, filed per the resolved layout (config-driven,
  ADR-007) and indexed. `--kind` auto-detects content-type. **`/wiki-sync`** is the
  batch **driver** for a *mixed* folder: it classifies every file and delegates each
  distil source to `/wiki-import` (no separate summarise/convert path). **Owns the markdown.**
- **Index layer (`/wiki-*` from obsidian-llm-wiki)** — fast read paths:
  FTS5 search across vaults, SQL-level lint, rebuildable cache. **Reads
  what the construct layer writes.**

> **Concept pages compound automatically.** A concept page carries a derived
> "Mentions across sources" ledger (`<!-- BEGIN-AUTO:mentions -->` … block) listing
> every source that references it — a rebuildable Class-B render, regenerated by
> `/wiki-index-render --concept-mentions` (TASK 047). You never hand-merge it.

### Lookup priority

When looking up domain facts, concepts, entities, or prior decisions:

1. **First** — `/wiki-search "<query>" --vaults ${vault_id}` (FTS5, < 100 ms).
2. **Then** — open the matched page(s) via `[[wiki-link]]` in Obsidian or
   `Read` the file.
3. **Only then** — fall back to `grep` / filesystem walk.

This is what makes the wiki *compounding*: searches return synthesis pages,
not raw transcripts.

### 1. Ingest a new source

**An external source (a URL / PDF / X-thread / meeting transcript)** — use
`/wiki-import`: it fetches + converts deterministically, hands you the cleaned
text + the vault's `known_concepts` for the REASON step, then files a summary note
and its `_concepts/` per this vault's layout.

```bash
/wiki-import prepare --vault ${vault_id} --vault-root . --kind auto \
             --source "https://example.com/article"
#   …summarise reusing the emitted known_concepts, then:
/wiki-import apply   --vault ${vault_id} --vault-root . --kind "<prepare.kind>" --note-stdin
```

**A local raw file already in the tree** — the *same* `/wiki-import`: point
`--source` at the file instead of a URL. It distils and files it per the layout —
note **plus** `_concepts/`, indexed in one shot — exactly like an external source
(there is no separate local-file command).

```bash
/wiki-import prepare --vault ${vault_id} --vault-root . --kind auto \
             --source ./path/to/source.md
#   …summarise reusing the emitted known_concepts, then `apply` as above.
```

A single import typically touches **10–15 wiki pages**. Stay involved: read
the generated summary, sanity-check the concept pages it filed, intervene when
Claude over- or under-extracts.

### 2. Query — answer from the wiki

```bash
# fast path — FTS5 hits + snippets
/wiki-search "what is X" --vaults ${vault_id}

# RAG: retrieve → orchestrator-cited synthesis → file a compounding _queries/<slug>.md
/wiki-query prepare "compare X and Y" --vault ${vault_id} --vault-root .
#   (orchestrator synthesises a cited answer, then) /wiki-query apply …
# Optional audit: /wiki-verify-multi prepare/apply  (off-by-default 4-critic check)
```

Every claim in an answer should carry a `[^src-<slug>]` footnote (or a `cited:`
ref) that resolves to a `_sources/` page — so it traces back to the raw source.

**Retrieval-scope controls (optional — ADR-009, all default-OFF).** Each is OFF until
opted into (the pages a query returns are then unchanged; the only always-on addition is a
`trust` tier on each `/wiki-query prepare` hit). They scope *what the model sees* — least-privilege
for cooperating agents, **not** protection from someone with file/DB access.

```bash
# Provenance floor: hits are tiered external (http(s) source:/url:/URL: or _raw/) < internal
# < verified (inbound `verifies` edge); origin taints. --min-trust grounds RAG on trusted pages:
/wiki-query prepare "..." --vault ${vault_id} --vault-root . --min-trust internal
# Classification scope: declare `policy: {levels, default_level}` in WIKI_SCHEMA.md, mark pages
# `classification: <level>`; higher-than-audience pages never enter context (fail-closed):
/wiki-search "..." --vaults ${vault_id} --audience internal      # also on /wiki-query, /wiki-verify-multi
# Read-audit: attribute multi-agent writes/reads (solo use rarely needs it):
WIKI_ACTOR_ID=critic-a /wiki-query apply ...                     # stamps details.actor on the log_events row
```

`/wiki-lint` flags out-of-ladder labels (`invalid-classification`, a warning) and a lower→higher
cite (`classification-leak`, an error under `--strict`); a bad `--audience`/`policy:` block exits 2.
`apply` must repeat the SAME `--audience`/`--min-trust` flags as `prepare` (they fold into
`question_hash` → `QUESTION_CHANGED` on mismatch). The high-value control for a single owner is
`--min-trust`; `--audience`/read-audit earn their keep under shared or multi-agent use.

### 3. Lint — health-check

```bash
# SQL-level (orphan links, hash mismatch, cross-vault dups + lifecycle-drift + ontology-violation)
/wiki-lint --vault ${vault_id}              # --strict to gate on lifecycle-drift + ontology-violation

# Derived knowledge health — pages MISSING an expected relation (typed-class vaults; exit 0)
/wiki-health coverage --vault ${vault_id}
# Ontology-contract report — pages CONTRADICTING the declared type/edge/property contract (cybos; exit 0)
/wiki-health ontology --vault ${vault_id}
```

Run periodically. `/wiki-lint` catches drift between markdown and the DB (orphan
links, hash mismatch, cross-vault dups) plus two config-driven *contradictions* that
gate `--strict`: **lifecycle-drift** (authored `status` vs the graph) and
**ontology-violation** (an edge whose source/target class or a `status` value breaks the
declared `ontology:` contract — cybos layouts). `/wiki-health` is the always-exit-0
report view: `coverage` (a typed page missing an expected relation) and `ontology`.

### 4. Rebuild — when in doubt

```bash
# Rebuild the SQLite cache from canonical markdown.
# Safe: ADR-002 §D8 makes the DB fully rebuildable from files.
# `wiki-reindex --full` wipes + rebuilds THIS vault's rows from Class-A markdown and
# resolves the declared index_db — no manual DB file deletion needed (TASK 025).
/wiki-init --register-existing --vault .
/wiki-reindex --full --vault ${vault_id}
```

### 5. Render — regenerate `index.md`

```bash
/wiki-index-render --vault ${vault_id}
```

Custom sections marked `<!-- BEGIN-CUSTOM:name -->` are preserved across
re-renders.

### 6. Promote / Demote (per-project layout only)

When `lint` flags that the same canonical name exists in `_concepts/` of
≥ 2 courses *and* you've confirmed they describe the same thing, **promote** it
to the shared root tier: relocate `Lessons/<Course>/_concepts/<Name>.md` →
root `_concepts/<Name>.md` (use `obsidian-cli move` so backlinks are rewritten,
not orphaned), then `/wiki-reindex --delta --vault ${vault_id}`. Demote is the
inverse move back into a course tier. See `WIKI_SCHEMA.md` for the promotion rules.

### 7. Auto-sync a mixed zone (`/wiki-sync`)

For a *mixed* folder — transcripts, office/PDF docs, ready notes, and Obsidian
generated-view sidecars all together — `/wiki-sync` is a **format-aware,
tag-routed dispatcher** that auto-routes each file instead of hand-invoking
`/wiki-import` per file. `scan` is deterministic (emits a plan; no LLM/mutation);
the orchestrator then executes it with per-file idempotency.

```bash
# preview the plan (writes nothing): per-file action + reason + counts
/wiki-sync scan "<zone>" --vault ${vault_id} --dry-run
#   then execute the plan via workflows/wiki-sync.md (per-file isolation, re-run no-op)
```

How each file is routed:

- **transcripts** (`.vtt`/`.srt`/`.txt`) → de-timestamp → delegate to `/wiki-import`
  (distil → summary note + `_concepts/`).
- **office / PDF** (`.docx`/`.pptx`/`.xlsx`/`.pdf`) → convert → `/wiki-import`; a
  **scanned/image-only PDF is OCR'd** (`pdf_ocr.py`, eng+rus) before import.
- **ready `.md`** (a mappable note) → `wiki-index-upsert` as-is.
- **generated-view sidecars** (DB Folder / Bases / Dataview / folder-notes) →
  **auto-skipped** (navigation, not knowledge).

Per-note tags override the default (precedence **skip > raw > keep > default**):
`#wiki/raw` = force full ingest (distil this note), `#wiki/skip` = never touch
(drafts), `#wiki/keep` = rescue a note from an excluded zone. Configure zones /
exclusions / tag namespace in `<vault>/.wiki/sync.yaml`.

